Let’s visualize what happens when `ss >> percent` fails:

```
Input Stream State
+---------------------+
| Buffer: "X% A"      |
| (character 'X' is invalid)|
+---------^-------------+
          |
          v
        Failbit set
```

When extraction fails, the stream enters a failed state. You can check this state using:

```cpp
if (!ss) {
    std::cerr << "Failed to extract an integer.\n";
}
```

or

```cpp
if (ss.fail()) {
    // The same as !ss — both check failbit
}
```

The `!ss` expression returns true when the stream is in a failed state. This happens if no matching data was found or conversion failed.

Now, what would happen if you tried to extract another value *after* this failure? Would it work?

