Let's clarify the state of `std::stringstream` after extraction:

Suppose your input is "90 A" — the stream successfully extracts both values. At that point, `ss.fail()` returns false and `percent == 90`, `grade == 'A'`. The string has been consumed up to the space.

Now consider what happens when you call `!ss.eof()`: this checks whether the end of file (EOF) has been reached — not whether there's garbage left in the buffer. Since no newline or EOF character was encountered, `eof()` returns false → `!ss.eof()` is true.

But here's the key: even if the input were "90 A ", whitespace characters *are* consumed by `>>` operators. So your current check won't catch extra spaces — only real garbage like digits after 'A'.

However, there's a better way to detect whether any characters remain unextracted:

After reading both values, you can use:
  ss >> ws; // skips whitespace
  char c = 'X';
  if (ss >> c) {
    printf("Unexpected character: %c\n", c);
  }

This ensures that only fully parsed inputs with *no* trailing characters pass.

Also — a quick note on formatting: your code has inconsistent spacing around operators and missing spaces after commas in function arguments. Please use consistent indentation and add spaces (e.g., `percent < 0` → `percent < 0`). This improves readability and avoids style deductions.
